Shell - a program that helps operate the computer by starting other programs offering a CLI 
examples of shells are sh, csh, ksh, bash(bourne again), fish(friendly, interactive), zsh(macOS), pwsh(Windows), dash(Debian) 

bash - command lang interpreter the executes commands read from stdin / string / file

Shell grammar
Simple command 
- optional var assignments followed by blank-separated words and redirections, terminated by control operator
- first word specifies the commmand to be executed - remaining words are passed as args to the invoked command
- return value is exit status or 128 + n if terminated by signal n 

Pipeline
sequence of one or more commands separated by | or |& 

format for a pipeline is:

              [time [-p]] [ ! ] command1 [ [|⎪|&] command2 ... ]

std output of command1 is fed via pipe to stdin of command2 - this is done before any redirections specified by commmand1

Each command in a multi-command pipeline, where pipes are created, is executed in a subshell, i.e. a separate process

List
sequence of one or more pipelines separated by one of the operators ;,&,&&,|| and optionally terminated by ; & \n
of these && and || have equal precedence followed by ; and & which have equal precedence
if a commmand is terminated by &, shell executes the command in the background in a subshell - the shell does not wait for the command to finish and return status is 0
These are called asynchronous commands

commands separated by ; are executed sequentially; shell waits for each command to terminate in turn and return status is the exit status of the last command executed

AND and OR lists are sequences of one or more pipelines separated by the && and || control operators - executed with left associativity 

command1 && command2 
command2 is executed if and only if command1 return exit status 0 (success)

command1 || command2 
command2 is executed if and only if command1 returns a non-zero exit status

return status of AND and OR lists is the exit status of the last command executed

Quoting - used to remove the special meaning of certain chars to the shell - can be used to disable spl treatment for spl chars, to prevent reserved words from being recognised as such, and to prevent parameter expansion
4 quoting mechanisms
a. escape char (\) - preserves literal value of the next char that follows, removing any spl meaning except \n If a
       \<newline> pair appears, and the backslash is not itself quoted,
       the \<newline> is treated as a line continuation (that is, it is
       removed from the input stream and effectively ignored).
b. single quotes - preserves literal value of all chars within the quotes - A single quote may not occur
       between single quotes, even when preceded by a backslash.
c. double quotes - preserves the literal value
       of all characters within the quotes, with the exception of $, `,
       \, and, when history expansion is enabled, !. A double quote may be quoted within double quotes by preceding it
       with a backslash. 
d. dollar-single quotes

