The global variable will be a state flag shared between main shell loop and signal handlers
SIGINT / SIGQUIT are async fns that can be called anytime but our signal handler must stay minimal (can't be calling malloc, readline etc inside it)
So, handler will just:
Set a global flag -> g_signal_received = SIGINT;
& Return control to the main loop

Then, in the main loop / executor, check flag to decide how to react
say cancel heredoc input, redisplay prompt, update exit status etc.

Normal prompt and heredoc mode will handle SIGINT / SIGQUIT differently:

Normal 
- SIGINT - cancel current i/p line, print \n, redisplay prompt, set exit status $? to 130
- SIGQUIT - just ignore it

Heredoc 
SIGINT - abort heredoc / close heredoc pipe / exit child with 130 / parent sees this and skips command execution
SIGQUIT - just ignore it

global flag is for the main loop
heredoc child process needs its own signal handler - it tells the parent to cancel via exit code

Heredoc - short for here document - block of text directly to a command's STDIN inside the shell script without needing an external file
cat << EOF
hello 
world
EOF

here, instead of cat reading from STDIN 
<< EOF tells shell - don't wait for STDIN - collect everything typed until a line with EOF is found
shell needs to store text b/w delimiters and feeds it into cat as if coming from a file / stdin

Variants
<< heredoc (with var expansion by default)
<<- heredoc (strips leading tabs)
<<'EOF' quoted heredoc (no var expansion inside)

Lexer produces tokens / just knows heredoc redirect coming, delimiter is EOF
cat - word
<< - TOKEN_REDIRECT_HEREDOC
EOF - word (delimiter)

Parser takes tokens and builds an AST
sees command node cat with a redirection node (type HEREDOC, delimiter EOF)
heredoc body hasn't been read still - parser just knows it must be handled before execution

Preprocessing heredocs before execution
For every heredoc redirection in AST, minishell:
- will fork a small child (or stay in parent)
- repeated calls to readline
- collect user i/p until delimiter (EOF) sequence
- write everything collected to a pipe
- store the read end of the pipe (heredoc_fd) in the command structure

Now, the fd is enriched with the actual fd to feed into stdin during execution

Execution Phase
Executor runs cat
sees a redirection - stdin must from cmd->heredoc_fd
dup2(cmd-heredoc_fd, STDIN_FILENO);
close(cmd->heredoc_fd)

Then it runs execve("cat",...)

Result - cat reads from heredoc pipe instead of waiting for user typing

Lexer → detects << and the delimiter token.
Parser → attaches a heredoc redirection node to the command in the AST.
Preprocessor → reads user input until delimiter, stores it in a pipe.
Executor → sets up stdin redirection from that pipe, then executes the command.

